DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The routines described in this page provide the application access to the
     system network configuration database, _/_e_t_c_/_n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g, as it is
     “filtered” by the NETPATH environment variable (see environ(7)).  See
     getnetconfig(3) for other routines that also access the network configu‐
     ration database directly.  The NETPATH variable is a list of colon-sepa‐
     rated network identifiers.

     The ggeettnneettppaatthh() function returns a pointer to the netconfig database en‐
     try corresponding to the first valid NETPATH component.  The netconfig
     entry is formatted as a _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g.  On each subsequent call,
     ggeettnneettppaatthh() returns a pointer to the netconfig entry that corresponds to
     the next valid NETPATH component.  The ggeettnneettppaatthh() function can thus be
     used to search the netconfig database for all networks included in the
     NETPATH variable.  When NETPATH has been exhausted, ggeettnneettppaatthh() returns
     NULL.

     A call to sseettnneettppaatthh() “binds” to or “rewinds” NETPATH.  The sseettnneettppaatthh()
     function must be called before the first call to ggeettnneettppaatthh() and may be
     called at any other time.  It returns a handle that is used by
     ggeettnneettppaatthh().

     The ggeettnneettppaatthh() function silently ignores invalid NETPATH components.  A
     NETPATH component is invalid if there is no corresponding entry in the
     netconfig database.

     If the NETPATH variable is unset, ggeettnneettppaatthh() behaves as if NETPATH were
     set to the sequence of “default” or “visible” networks in the netconfig
     database, in the order in which they are listed.

     The eennddnneettppaatthh() function may be called to “unbind” from NETPATH when
     processing is complete, releasing resources for reuse.  Programmers
     should be aware, however, that eennddnneettppaatthh() frees all memory allocated by
     ggeettnneettppaatthh() for the struct netconfig data structure.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseettnneettppaatthh() function returns a handle that is used by ggeettnneettppaatthh().
     In case of an error, sseettnneettppaatthh() returns NULL.

     The eennddnneettppaatthh() function returns 0 on success and -1 on failure (for ex‐
     ample, if sseettnneettppaatthh() was not called previously).  The nncc__ppeerrrroorr() or
     nncc__ssppeerrrroorr() function can be used to print out the reason for failure.
     See getnetconfig(3).

     When first called, ggeettnneettppaatthh() returns a pointer to the netconfig data‐
     base entry corresponding to the first valid NETPATH component.  When
     NETPATH has been exhausted, ggeettnneettppaatthh() returns NULL.

